Thanks for the follow-up, the declarative output is much better than a reference to a generated id.

Keeping the argument alive past `ResolveLazyProxyPass` means the places that assumed it had become a plain `Reference` by then need to be taught about it. I found four and pushed the fixes with tests:

- `ResolveInvalidReferencesPass` pushed `getValues()` through its generic array handler, which nulls or unsets entries and re-indexes. On a lazy proxy pointing at a service tagged `container.excluded` with `nullOnInvalid()` or `ignoreOnInvalid()`, that assigned the interfaces array (or null) to `$service` and compilation died with `TypeError: Cannot assign array to property LazyProxyArgument::$service of type Reference`. It now degrades like the reference it wraps, so the argument becomes null instead.
- `PhpDumper` only builds the lazily loaded service-locator entry `['privates', id, method, shared]` when the value is a `Reference`. A lazy proxy fell to the eager form, so every proxy in a locator was built when the locator was constructed rather than on `get()`. It now unwraps to the resolved reference and the dump is identical to before.
- `ResolveHotPathPass` stops at any `ArgumentInterface`, so `container.hot_path` no longer reached the generated `.lazy.*` definition nor the proxied service. That definition is still instantiated eagerly when the consumer is built, so it belongs on the hot path; the pass now follows the resolved reference.
- `TextDescriptor` prints iterator children with `sprintf('- Service(%s)', $ref)`. With `!iterator [ '@~a' ]`, `debug:container` died with `Object of class LazyProxyArgument could not be converted to string`. The formatting is now shared between the top-level and nested cases.

Also added a lazy proxy to `ObjectsProvider`, both at the top level and inside the existing iterator, so the three descriptors are covered by fixtures.

Verified the compiled container is unchanged against 8.2 for fifteen scenarios (interfaces, aliases, decorators, method calls, invalid behaviors, non-shared targets, several consumers, nesting in iterators, locators and plain arrays).
